{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"nodejs-essentials","title":"Node.js Essentials","group":"Browser & Node.js","order":58,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Node.js is V8 plus libuv plus a standard library for files, networking, processes and streams — JavaScript outside the browser.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cpu\"></i>Architecture</h3>\n<div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"flow-node\">Your JS</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">V8 (executes)</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">Node bindings</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">libuv (event loop + thread pool)</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">OS</div></div>\n<ul><li>Network I/O is non-blocking at the OS level; file-system, DNS lookups, crypto and zlib use libuv's thread pool (4 threads by default).</li><li>One JS thread per process; scale with <code>cluster</code>, <code>worker_threads</code>, or multiple processes behind a load balancer.</li><li>Globals: <code>process</code>, <code>Buffer</code>, <code>globalThis</code>, <code>setImmediate</code>, <code>structuredClone</code>, <code>fetch</code>; no <code>window</code> or <code>document</code>.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-route\"></i>path and process</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>import path from \"node:path\";\nconst file = \"/home/asha/projects/app/src/index.test.js\";\nconsole.log(path.basename(file), path.extname(file), path.dirname(file));\nconsole.log(path.join(\"/a/b\", \"../c\", \"d.txt\"), path.resolve(\"/root\", \"x\", \"..\", \"y\"));\nconsole.log(path.parse(\"/tmp/report.pdf\"));\nconsole.log(typeof process.argv, process.argv.length &gt;= 2, process.platform.length &gt; 0);\nconst port = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 3000);          // env vars are strings or undefined\nconsole.log(port, typeof process.exitCode);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>index.test.js .js /home/asha/projects/app/src\n/a/c/d.txt /root/y\n{\n  root: '/',\n  dir: '/tmp',\n  base: 'report.pdf',\n  ext: '.pdf',\n  name: 'report'\n}\nobject true true\n3000 undefined</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-files\"></i>The fs module (promises API)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>import { writeFile, readFile, appendFile, readdir, mkdir, rm, stat } from \"node:fs/promises\";\nimport { existsSync } from \"node:fs\";\n\nawait mkdir(\"data/logs\", { recursive: true });\nawait writeFile(\"data/logs/app.log\", \"started\\n\");\nawait appendFile(\"data/logs/app.log\", \"ready\\n\");\nconst text = await readFile(\"data/logs/app.log\", \"utf8\");\nconsole.log(text.trim().split(\"\\n\"));\nconsole.log(await readdir(\"data\"), (await stat(\"data/logs/app.log\")).size);\ntry {\n  await readFile(\"missing.txt\", \"utf8\");\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.code);\n}\nawait rm(\"data\", { recursive: true });\nconsole.log(existsSync(\"data\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 'started', 'ready' ]\n[ 'logs' ] 14\nENOENT\nfalse</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Use <code>node:fs/promises</code> with <code>await</code> in application code. The <code>*Sync</code> variants block the event loop — fine in CLI scripts and at startup, harmful inside a server request handler.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-server\"></i>A minimal HTTP server</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>import http from \"node:http\";\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.writeHead(200, { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" });\n  res.end(JSON.stringify({ method: req.method, path: req.url }));\n});\nserver.listen(0, async () =&gt; {\n  const { port } = server.address();\n  const body = await (await fetch(`http://localhost:${port}/hello?x=1`)).json();\n  console.log(body);\n  server.close();\n});</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{ method: 'GET', path: '/hello?x=1' }</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i>npm and package.json</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ npm init -y                    # create package.json\n$ npm install express            # dependency  → \"dependencies\"\n$ npm install -D vitest          # dev-only    → \"devDependencies\"\n$ npm run test                   # run a script from \"scripts\"\n$ npx eslint .                   # run a package binary without installing globally\n$ npm ci                         # clean, exact install from package-lock.json (CI)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Version range</th><th>Accepts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>\"4.18.2\"</code></td><td>Exactly that version</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"^4.18.2\"</code></td><td><code>&gt;=4.18.2 &lt;5.0.0</code> — minor + patch updates (npm's default)</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"~4.18.2\"</code></td><td><code>&gt;=4.18.2 &lt;4.19.0</code> — patch updates only</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is Node.js single-threaded?</summary><div><p>Your JavaScript runs on a single main thread with an event loop, but Node itself uses libuv's thread pool for file I/O, DNS, crypto and compression, and you can create more JS threads with <code>worker_threads</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}